proofs.proof_tex:
Fix a cell and one evaluation fold.  Conditional on its opposite training fold, the estimated adjoint score is fixed and the observations in the evaluation fold are independent.  The centering by the evaluation-fold mean changes a raw second moment into its empirical covariance and contributes only the usual squared empirical mean.  On the good event in lem:uniform-critical-estimates, its fourth-moment bound gives, entry by entry,
\[
 \operatorname{Var}_P\!\left[
 L_n^{-1}\frac n{n_g}\frac1{n_g}
 \sum_{i=1}^{n_g}
 \widehat\Psi_{g,h_n}^{(-r(i))}(X_{g,i})
 \widehat\Psi_{g,h_n}^{(-r(i))}(X_{g,i})^{\mathsf T}
 \ \middle|\ \text{training fold}\right]
 \leq\frac{C}{nh_n^2L_n^2}=o(1).
\tag{1}
\]
The same calculation shows that empirical centering has \(o_{\mathbb P}(1)\) effect after division by \(L_n\).  Apply (1) separately to the two folds; this is valid although either fold is used to train the other, because each assertion is conditioned only while its opposite fold is evaluated.  A union bound then gives the conditional-law-of-large-numbers conclusion for their sum.

Let \(\Delta_{g,r}=\widehat\Psi_{g,h_n}^{(-r)}-\Psi_{g,h_n}\).  By Cauchy--Schwarz and lem:uniform-critical-estimates,
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 &L_n^{-1}\left\lVert
 E_{P_g}[\widehat\Psi\widehat\Psi^{\mathsf T}]
 -E_{P_g}[\Psi\Psi^{\mathsf T}]
 \right\rVert_{\mathrm{op}}\\
 &\quad\leq L_n^{-1}\lVert\Delta_{g,r}\rVert_2
 \{2\lVert\Psi_{g,h_n}\rVert_2+\lVert\Delta_{g,r}\rVert_2\}
 =o_{\mathbb P}(1),
 \end{aligned}
\tag{2}
\]
uniformly in \(P,g,r\).  The identical bound applies to the squared means.  Since \(n/n_g\to\rho_g^{-1}\), equations (1)--(2) and def:full-score-covariance imply
\[
 \lVert\widehat V_{\mathrm{adj}}-\Sigma_{h_n}(P)\rVert_{\mathrm{op}}
 =o_{\mathbb P}(1)
\tag{3}
\]
uniformly.  Lem:full-critical-covariance turns (3) into the first asserted covariance consistency statement.

Because \(\theta_n=n^{-1}\),
\[
 \lVert\widehat V_{\mathrm{adj}}^R-V(P)\rVert_{\mathrm{op}}
 \leq\lVert\widehat V_{\mathrm{adj}}-V(P)\rVert_{\mathrm{op}}+n^{-1},
\tag{4}
\]
which proves the second.  The matrix \(\widehat V_{\mathrm{adj}}\) is positive semidefinite by construction, so \(\widehat V_{\mathrm{adj}}^R\succeq n^{-1}I_{2J}\) in every sample.  Finally, (6) in lem:full-critical-covariance supplies a uniform lower eigenvalue bound for every \(V_j(P)\).  On the event
\(\lVert\widehat V_{mathrm{adj}}^R-V(P)\rVert_{\mathrm{op}}<\lambda_{\min}(V(P))/2\), the resolvent identity gives
\[
 \left\lVert(\widehat V_{mathrm{adj},jj}^R)^{-1}-V_j(P)^{-1}\right\rVert_{mathrm{op}}
 \leq 2\lambda_{\min}(V(P))^{-2}
 \lVert\widehat V_{mathrm{adj},jj}^R-V_j(P)\rVert_{mathrm{op}}.
\tag{5}
\]
Equations (3)--(5), and finiteness of \(J\), prove the block-inverse conclusion.  All harmonic and generated-target cross-products are retained in (1); thm:nested-derivative and lem:uniform-critical-estimates merely show why their normalized limiting contributions vanish.

The matrix \(\widehat V_{\mathrm{adj}}\) is a sum of outer products and is therefore positive semidefinite.  Since \(\theta_n=n^{-1}>0\),
\[
 \widehat V_{\mathrm{adj}}^R=\widehat V_{\mathrm{adj}}+n^{-1}I_{2J}
 \succ0
\tag{1}
\]
for every sample.  Thus every principal block is invertible.  Conditional on the data, independence and Gaussianity of the multipliers give the exact identity
\[
 Z^\star\mid\text{data}\sim N(0,\widehat V_{\mathrm{adj}}).
\tag{2}
\]
Consequently the displayed maximum is a finite nonnegative random variable and its lower conditional \((1-\gamma)\)-quantile is well defined in \([0,\infty)\), even when the conditional covariance is singular.

By thm:adjoint-covariance and its block-inverse conclusion, the conditional characteristic function in (2) converges uniformly on compact sets, in probability and uniformly over \(P\in\mathcal F_s\), to that of \(Z\sim N(0,V(P))\).  Hence, conditionally on the data,
\[
 \max_{1\leq j\leq J}(Z_j^\star)^{\mathsf T}
 (\widehat V_{mathrm{adj},jj}^R)^{-1}Z_j^\star
 \Rightarrow
 \max_{1\leq j\leq J}Z_j^{\mathsf T}V_j(P)^{-1}Z_j.
\tag{3}
\]
Lem:full-critical-covariance makes \(V(P)\) block diagonal and positive definite.  The right side of (3) is therefore the maximum of \(J\) independent \(\chi^2_2\) variables.  Its distribution function is continuous and strictly increasing on \((0,\infty)\); thus conditional quantiles converge to its unique \((1-\gamma)\)-quantile.

On the other hand, thm:joint-critical-clt, thm:adjoint-covariance, and the ordinary resolvent identity give
\[
 \max_{1\leq j\leq J}\frac n{L_n}
 (\widehat\tau_j-\tau_j(P))^{\mathsf T}
 (\widehat V_{mathrm{adj},jj}^R)^{-1}
 (\widehat\tau_j-\tau_j(P))
 \Rightarrow
 \max_{1\leq j\leq J}\chi^2_{2,j}
\tag{4}
\]
uniformly over \(P\in\mathcal F_s\).  Combining the quantile convergence from (3) with (4) and continuity of the limiting distribution proves
\[
 \inf_{P\in\mathcal F_s}\mathbb P_P
 \{\tau_j(P)\in\mathcal E_j\text{ for every }1\leq j\leq J\}
 \longrightarrow1-\gamma.
\tag{5}
\]
This proves both finite-sample totality and simultaneous asymptotic coverage.
